WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.657
An AI inference cluster is an operational system, not simply a collection of GPUs.

00:00:05.657 --> 00:00:10.024
Its design begins with users, models, data sensitivity, latency, throughput, concurrency,

00:00:10.024 --> 00:00:11.215
uptime, and budget.

00:00:11.215 --> 00:00:15.185
Those requirements determine whether a cluster is justified at all.

00:00:15.185 --> 00:00:19.948
A single server remains the better answer when it can meet demand

00:00:19.948 --> 00:00:22.727
with acceptable recovery and less management overhead.

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:27.093
When scale-out is justified, choose the simplest architecture that satisfies it.

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:30.269
Use stable model servers behind a controlled interface,

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:33.048
route requests only to compatible healthy nodes,

00:00:33.048 --> 00:00:35.827
and make queueing and overload behavior explicit.

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:41.384
Match GPUs and VRAM to model roles, support them with sufficient CPU, RAM, storage,

00:00:41.384 --> 00:00:43.369
network capacity, power, and cooling,

00:00:43.369 --> 00:00:48.132
and understand where consumer components trade lower cost for fewer operational features.

00:00:48.132 --> 00:00:52.499
Manage model weights, tokenizers, templates, runtimes, and configuration as versioned releases.

00:00:52.499 --> 00:00:57.659
Keep active models in local worker caches when that improves startup and resilience,

00:00:57.659 --> 00:01:00.438
while maintaining an approved source of truth.

00:01:00.438 --> 00:01:04.408
Separate those artifacts from prompts, embeddings, logs, and private datasets.

00:01:04.408 --> 00:01:07.981
Back up irreplaceable configuration and data, and test recovery.

00:01:07.981 --> 00:01:10.759
Finally, secure and observe the whole service.

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:14.332
Authenticate clients, segment management paths, protect secrets, control patches,

00:01:14.332 --> 00:01:17.508
and monitor both user experience and hardware health.

00:01:17.508 --> 00:01:20.683
Build rollback and incident response into routine operations.

00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:23.859
Start with measured demand, establish a repeatable baseline,

00:01:23.859 --> 00:01:27.829
and scale the component that evidence identifies as the bottleneck.

00:01:27.829 --> 00:01:34.180
That discipline produces a cluster that delivers dependable capacity instead of multiplying cost and failure modes.
